# Chapter 5 of the Reference Manual: Markers.  The chapter is kept in several
# files that this one includes: what a marker is, the table of all the
# markers, panels, screen and print (markers1); the markers from Author to
# Equation (markers2); Filedate to Keycap (markers3); LaTeX to Printer
# (markers4); Revision to Whitespace and the markers printed as a Box
# (markers5); the messages (markers6); the Print Error marker (markers7).
.doc Strata Reference: Markers
.chapter 5
.h1 Markers [markers]
.ix Markers
.ix Marker table
.ix Alt-F12
A marker is an item that is put into the text in place of a letter: a date
that is filled in when the document is printed, a footnote, a page break, a
picture, a counter that numbers the items of a list.  A marker has a panel of
its own.  {k:Alt-F12} or {k:Ctrl-F12} puts a marker in, the same key with the
cursor on a marker changes it, and {k:Del} takes it out like a letter
({n:edit-b-marker}).  The Marker settings of the Global menu set how a marker
looks on the screen ({n:format-marker}).  This chapter says what each marker
is, what each field of its panel means and what the marker does on the screen
and on paper.
.table 20 40
Section | What it covers
{x:markers-what} | What a marker is and where it can stand.
{x:markers-table} | All the markers in one table, with the default display and
 the print.
{x:markers-panels} | The panels and the kinds of field.
{x:markers-screen} | The display modes and the label of each marker in each
 mode.
{x:markers-printed} | Which markers are typeset, which print a text, which are
 printed as a boxed label and which are not printed.
{x:markers-messages} | The messages of the marker commands and panels.
{x:markers-errors} | The Print Error marker, which only the Editor makes.
.end
The entries follow the order of the table Marker Commands, which is
alphabetical.  The markers that are printed as a Box have one entry together
({n:markers-labels}); the entries of Include and of the conditional markers
come last.  Every entry has the same parts:
- a first paragraph that says what the marker is for;
- a table of the panel with every field, the values that it accepts, its
  default and what it does;
- the look of the marker on the screen;
- what the Printer does with it;
- the edges: an empty field, a file that is not there, a marker where it does
  not belong;
- the messages that can be shown, and the other places of the manual to read.
